// unload_probe.nvgt - does the crash need a plugin call at all?
//
// The e2e script dies on this runner with the debugger naming the address
// rather than a function:
//
//   ExceptionAddress: 00007ffe991015e6 (<Unloaded_nvmidi.dll>+0x15e6)
//   ExceptionCode:    c0000005 (Access violation)
//   Parameter[0]:     8   (EXCEPTION_EXECUTE_FAULT - execute on non-executable)
//   Attempt to execute non-executable address
//
// and the stack above the fault is our own code, with the frame just outside
// the plugin being midi_output_port_count:
//
//   nvmidi!midi_output_port_count+0xc2
//   nvmidi!midi_export_backend_byte+0x11bec
//   nvmidi!midi_export_backend_byte+0x11aa3
//   nvmidi!midi_export_backend_byte+0x118df
//
// The faulting address is *inside the unloaded image*. That is the whole
// finding and it is read two ways, so this file exists to separate them:
//
//   a) the engine keeps a call into the plugin past the unload, and the
//      address it jumps to is in a page windows has already released - a
//      defect of the engine's unload order, not of anything this repo owns;
//   b) enumeration reaches into winmm, winmm calls back into a callback that
//      lives in the plugin, the plugin is unloaded first, and the callback
//      fires afterwards - a real lifetime bug in how the plugin hands winmm
//      a function pointer it does not retract.
//
// The difference is decidable with one script, because this one calls the
// engine and nothing else. It asks for no port, opens nothing, enumerates
// nothing, and registers no callback. If it dies the same way, the call into
// the unloaded image cannot be anything winmm started - winmm was never
// spoken to - and (a) is the answer. If it exits clean, the fault belongs to
// the plugin's winmm-facing lifetime and (b) is.
//
// The pragma is what makes the plugin load, and the load is the subject: a
// script with no pragma never loads the library and would say nothing about
// its unload.
//
// Left in scripts/windows/ and named here rather than run once by hand: the
// answer decides which repo the fix belongs to, and a measurement that
// settles that should be repeatable by whoever reads this next.
#pragma plugin nvmidi

void main() {
	// One print, so a clean exit is distinguishable from a death before the
	// script ever ran. The engine buffers stdout and flushes at exit, so this
	// line may still arrive after the process is gone - but a crash before the
	// first print leaves the same empty file a refusal does, and the two are
	// told apart by the exit code the job publishes beside this log.
	print("UNLOAD_BEGIN");

	// The plugin's own api name, and nothing else. It is a plugin call for
	// certain - the string is built by the plugin and handed over as bytes -
	// so its answer proves the library is loaded and answering, without
	// touching a port, a device or a callback.
	const int bytes = midi_api_name_byte_count();
	print("UNLOAD_API_BYTES " + bytes);

	// No enumeration, no port, no note. The script is over here, and the
	// interesting part is what the engine does after this line with a plugin
	// that winmm has never been introduced to.
	print("UNLOAD_END");
}
